        "management": {
            "firstLine": {
                "immediate": [
                    "TEN is a dermatologic emergency requiring immediate hospitalization in an intensive care unit (ICU) or burn unit for multidisciplinary care, including wound management, fluid resuscitation, and organ support. \u2022 The first and most critical step is to immediately discontinue the offending drug. 432 o Fluid resuscitation is essential to prevent hypovolemia due to massive skin loss. o Electrolyte imbalances should be corrected through intravenous fluids and electrolyte replacement. o The denuded skin should be treated like burns, using non-adherent dressings and ensuring a sterile environment to prevent infection. o Topical antibiotics (e.g., silver sulfadiazine ) may be used to prevent secondary infection. o Enteral feeding is preferred, but parenteral nutrition may be required if oral intake is not possible due to mucosal involvement. o IVIG is used in some cases to neutralize autoantibodies and halt the immune response. o Dosage."
                ],
                "ongoing": [
                    "Typically 2 g/kg over 3-5 days. o IVIG is believed to inhibit Fas-mediated keratinocyte apoptosis, thus reducing further skin necrosis. o The use of systemic corticosteroids in TEN remains controversial, as they may increase the risk of infection. However, some studies suggest that early, short - term use of high-dose steroids may be beneficial in halting disease progression. o Cyclosporine (an immunosuppressant) may reduce immune -mediated destruction of keratinocytes. o Dosage.",
                    "3-5 mg/kg/day. \u2022 Antibiotics are not given prophylactically but are used if there is confirmed evidence of a secondary bacterial infection. \u2022 Prophylactic antibiotics should not be used unless there is evidence of secondary infection, as they may promote the development of antibiotic -resistant bacteria. o Plasmapheresis may be considered in severe cases to remove circulating autoantibodies and immune complexes. o In some centers, etanercept or infliximab may be used to inhibit tumour necrosis factor -alpha (TNF -\u03b1), a cytokine involved in the inflammatory response leading to epidermal necrosis. \u2022 Monitoring for organ involvement.",
                    "Regularly monitor renal, hepatic, and respiratory function, as TEN can lead to multiorgan failure."
                ]
            },
            "secondLine": [],
            "complications": [
                "May require long -term follow -up with ophthalmology and dermatology to manage scarring and prevent further complications. o The loss of the skin barrier increases the risk of bacterial infections, leading to sepsis, which is a leading cause of mortality in SJS. o SJS can affect multiple organs, including the kidneys, lungs, and liver, leading to renal failure, pneumonitis, and hepatitis. o Ocular complications.",
                "Conjunctivitis, corneal scarring, and blindness. o Skin.",
                "Scarring, pigmentation changes, and nail dystrophy. o Oral.",
                "Chronic mucosal ulceration and dental complications.",
                "Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis (TEN) is a rare, life -threatening disorder characterized by widespread epidermal detachment and mucosal involvement.",
                "It represents the severe end of the spectrum of drug-induced hypersensitivity reactions, with Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) being the milder form.",
                "TEN involves >30% of the total body surface area (TBSA), while SJS involves <10%, and SJS/TEN overlap occurs when 10-30% of the TBSA is involved.",
                "TEN is most often triggered by medications, leading to massive keratinocyte apoptosis and epidermal necrosis."
            ]
        },
